    },
    "message": "fix(desktop): uninstallers remove what the installers created (6.41.3)\n\n`uv tool uninstall` removes shims and nothing else, and purging ~/.mcc reaches\nonly what is inside the config directory. Everything the desktop half of an\ninstall writes lives outside both, so it all survived an uninstall: the Start\nMenu shortcut kept pointing at a deleted mcc-desktop.exe, and the HKCU Run\nvalue (or the LaunchAgent, or the systemd unit) kept relaunching a package that\nwas no longer installed.\n\nuninstall.ps1 now removes %APPDATA%\\...\\Start Menu\\Programs\\My Claude Code.lnk\n(New-DesktopShortcut, install.ps1:1096-1108) and the HKCU\n...\\CurrentVersion\\Run\\MyClaudeCodeDesktop value (_apply_windows_start_at_login,\nconfig/desktop.py:528). uninstall.sh removes the .desktop entry and its icon\n(create_linux_desktop_entry, install.sh:566), ~/Applications/My Claude Code.app\n(create_macos_app_bundle, install.sh:603), the LaunchAgent plist, the XDG\nautostart entry and the systemd user unit -- asking systemctl to forget the unit\nfirst, and only when the unit file we wrote is actually there, so a machine that\nnever enabled autostart (or has no user bus) is untouched.\n\nThe exported icon needs no new code: install.ps1 writes it to ~/.mcc/app-icon.ico\nand install.sh into the bundle, both already inside something that is removed.\n\nPurge semantics are unchanged: ~/.mcc and ~/.fcc go, ~/.fcc-old stays (it holds\nthe rollback note), and a new contract test pins that so the added removals\ncannot drift into it. Ordering is unchanged too -- the desktop artefacts are\nremoved only after every shim is verified gone, so an unverified tool removal\nstill leaves config *and* shortcut exactly as they were. A shortcut or registry\nvalue that cannot be deleted warns instead of aborting: the tool is already\ngone, and refusing there would strand the user with nothing to retry.\n\nTests: tests/contracts/test_uninstaller_parity.py gains an ARTEFACTS table\nnaming, for each artefact, the file that creates it and the file that must\nremove it -- with markers proving both still do, so adding a creator without a\nremover fails there. tests/scripts/test_uninstallers.py gains ten behavioural\nscenarios that lay the artefacts down and assert they are gone (and that\n--dry-run and an unverified removal leave them alone). The PowerShell fixture\nnow redirects APPDATA/LOCALAPPDATA and stubs Get-ItemProperty /\nRemove-ItemProperty, so no scenario can reach the real Start Menu or the real\nRun key.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JsSZ8AQXV1XKgKkgYBU5UT",
